## 3. Results and discussion
The formation of $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ catalysts involves two main steps as demonstrated in Fig. 1. N-doped graphene-like carbon nanosheets $({\mathsf{N@C s}})$ was firstly synthesized according to our previous report, [22] where soybean oil was used as carbon source and melamine was applied to form the layered sacrificial graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathsf{g}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ template during carbonization process (about 500–600 $^{\circ}C)$ [30,31]. Subsequently, the raw materials (metal salt and dimethylimidazole) for the formation of ZIFs were added into the dispersion liquid of obtained carbon nanosheets. Then, the organic metallic frameworks were gradually assembled onto the surface of these nanosheets via the interactions of functional groups. The ${\mathsf{F C@N C s}}$ catalysts were finally obtained through a secondary annealing process in ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere and their morphologies highly depend on the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ molar ratios.
The morphology features of obtained materials were studied through scanning electron microscope (SEM). The N@Cs sample displays a nanosheet structure with folded surface (Fig. S1). The $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ sample displays a leaf-like structure with small embosses in size of about $200\mathrm{nm}$ loaded on the leaf surface (Fig. 2- a-b). For the sample of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ (Fig. 2c-d), an interconnected network composed of grain-like micron-sized particles is observed. In the SEM images of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15}$ (Fig. 2e-f), the carbon sheets are curled into tubular-like structures due to the increase of ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{F e}}$ molar ratio. Thus, the morphology of the products highly depends on the initial Fe/Co molar ratio, which may affect the structure of obtained ZIF and the metal catalytic graphitization at high temperature. For the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Fig. 3), the three $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ samples display similar patterns. The dominant phase in $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ is bimetal CoFe alloy as the diffraction peaks at $44.7^{\circ}$ and $65.1^{\circ}$ are in agreement with the (110) and (200) facets of ${{\mathrm{C}}_{{0}_{3}}}$ - $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ (JCPDS No. 48-1817), respectively. The $44.2^{\circ}$ , $51.5^{\circ}$ , and $75.8^{\circ}$ diffraction peaks belong to the (111), (200), and (220) planes of $\upbeta$ -Co (JCPDS No. 15-0806), respectively [32]. Compared with the three samples, these peaks of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ are relatively strong and sharp, manifesting the better crystallinity of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ and $\upbeta$ -Co in FC@NCs-4.15. In addition, the (002) plane of graphite carbon (JCPDS No. 41-1487) is clearly observed at $26.38^{\circ}$ in the XRD pattern of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. S2) [33]. However, it seems to become inconspicuous in the three ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples, implying the change of previous layered structure in the resulted carbon matrix.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was applied to gain insight into the microstructure of different ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples. In the TEM images of the three samples (Fig. 4a and Fig. S3 a, d), black nanoparticles with sizes varing from about 10 to $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ are embedded in the carbon skeleton. The carbon structures are no longer similar as the graphene-like carbon nanosheets of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. S1). The carbon networks of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ and $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ are more integrated and continuous than that of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15}$ , which may affect the electrical conductivity of the material. In other word, the overmuch addition of Co source would reduce the integrality of the carbon substrate during the high temperature annealing. Some parts of the carbon substrate are curled into carbon nanotubes, especially in FC@NCs-3.15 and $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15}$ . The distribution of nanoparticles, especially these with diameter of $10{-}20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , in the carbon matrix of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ is much more uniform than in the other samples. An interplanar spacing with $0.202\ \mathrm{nm}$ is found in the HRTEM images (Fig. 4b-d and Fig. S3c) of the small nanoparticles (around $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), and these fingerprints can be indexed to the (110) facet of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ . On the other hand, the (111) plane of $\upbeta$ -Co with lattice spacing of $0.205~\mathrm{\nm}$ is observed in the HRTEM images (Fig. 4e and Fig. S3f) of large particles (e.g. $50\sim100\mathrm{nm}$ [34]. In addition, these small and large metal nanoparticles are wrapped by a graphene-like carbon layer with a thickness of about $3\ensuremath{-}5\ensuremath{\mathrm{~nm~}}$ (Fig. 4c-d, and Fig. $S3\mathrm{~c,~f)~}$ as the observed fingerprints of $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ belongs to the (0 0 2) facet of carbon. The corresponding energy-dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping was conducted to disclose surface element distribution of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ . Fig. 4g-h reveal that N and O elements are uniformly scattered in the carbonaceous network of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ and the signal strength is in the following order ${\mathsf C}>{\mathsf{O}}>{\mathsf{N}}$ . The O and N elements doping gives the credit to $\mathsf{O}/N$ -containing functional groups decorated on the catalysts surface [35]. Besides, the EDS signal of Co is much higher than that of Fe (Fig. 4j-k), which is consistent with their initial addition.
The porosity and specific surface area features of samples were investigated trough ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption experiments. The $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ samples (Fig. 5a) show similar isothermal curves to that of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. S4a), where an evident capillary condensation is observed at medium pressure of $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.43$ , implying the presence of mesopores in the samples [36]. In addition, $\mathsf{N@C s}$ shows much steeper $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption at high $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.98$ than the three ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples, suggesting $\mathsf{N@C s}$ has more large size pores. A H3 type hysteresis is found in all the isotherm curves, which means multi-layer stacking structure with low pore curvature exists in all the samples. In other word, the graphene-like structure can be partly maintained after introducing the metal sources into $\mathsf{N@C s}$ . The main difference between $\mathsf{N@C s}$ and ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ is that a steep $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption at low relative pressure can be found for the ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ , indicating there are also a small portion of micropores in these FC@NCs samples. The main pore sizes of FC@NCs are smaller than $4\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 5b) while $\mathsf{N@C s}$ has macropore distribution in addtion to mesopores (Fig. S4b). The pore volume and surface area of the materials are summarized in Table S1. As expected, ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples have larger surface area but smaller pore volume $(194.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.36~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ , $178.5\:\mathrm{~m}^{2}\:\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ $0.14~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ and $176.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.20\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ FC@NCs-5.15 compared with those of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ ( $142.9~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , $0.97\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}.$ ). The hierarchical pore structure and larger surface area of ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ are in favor of the diffusion of electrolyte and reactant intermediates in the electrocatalytic process [37–40].
X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were applied to examine the surface element constituents and their chemical states. The XPS survey spectra (Fig. S5) manifest the coexistence of O, N and C elements in the $\mathsf{N@C s}$ and ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples. Two small peaks at 710 and $780~\mathrm{eV}$ in the spectra of $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ could be attributed to Fe 2p and $\mathtt{C o2p}$ respectively, and the intensity of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ increases with the Fe/Co molar ratio changing from 1/3.15 to 1/5.15. The high resolution C1s spectra of ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ (Fig. 6a) and $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. S6a) can be fitted into three main peaks at about 284.6, 285.4 and $288.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ · confirming the existence of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}={\mathsf{C}},\ {\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{O}}/{\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{N}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds [41]. In addition, the percentage contributions of bonds at $285.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and $288.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ are larger than those in the other two samples, manifesting higher N or O-containing functional group content, which may work as active sites to capture oxygen during the ORR process [42–44]. This result is in consistent with the Raman spectra analysis (Fig. S7), where the intensity ratio $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ of lattice defect carbon (D-band, $1353~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and graphitic $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon (G-band, $1575~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) for FC@NCs-4.15 (0.93) is higher than 0.85 of FC@NCs-3.15 and 0.84 of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15}$ . The lower $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratio in $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ and $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15}$ may be caused by the formation of more carbon nanotubes (CNTs) due to the catalysis of transition metals as well as stronger 2D peaks than $\operatorname{FC@NCS-}$ 4.15 are found at about $2700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in their Raman spectra [45–48]. Compared with the Raman spectrum of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ , the $\mathrm{\DeltaI_{D}}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}$ of ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ are much lower due to the incorporation of metal source, which reduces their carbon content. The thermo-gravimetric (TG) analysis (Fig. S8) shows the carbon proportion of the samples is in following order: $97.2\%$ $(\mathsf{N@C S})>43.3\%$ $(\mathsf{F C@N C s-3.15})\ge41.5\%$ $(\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15},$ $>$ $37.2\%$ $\mathsf{'F C@N C s–4.15}$ . This result is consistent with the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) analysis (Table S2), which also shows that the $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}$ molar ratios in the three samples are close to the initial feed ratios. Thus, the $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}$ molar ratio for the initial MOF synthesis not only affects the carbon structure of the final products but also influences their carbon content. The less carbon content may be favorable to the exposure of reaction sites. For the divided N 1 s spectra of ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ and $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. 6b and Fig. S6b), four peaks at 398.2, 400.8, 401.7, and $404.8\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, graphitic N, and oxidized N, respectively [49–51]. Previous researches reveal that the graphitic N could enhance the conductivity of material for high limiting current density and pyridinic N could promote the onset potential of ORR [52–54]. For the three ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples, $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ has relatively higher graphitic N and pyridinic N contents, indicating $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ would show better ORR performance. The Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ XPS spectra of ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ can be divided into five main peaks around 706.5, 709.9, 714.2, 720.6 and 725.1 eV, assigned to $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}2\mathrm{p}3/2,\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}2\mathrm{p}3/2,\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}2\mathrm{p}3/2,\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ $2{\mathsf{p}}1/2$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}\ 2\mathsf{p}1/2$ , respectively. The zero valence iron proves the existence of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ while the oxidation state iron indicates the strong interaction between ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ and nearby atoms (e.g. C, N and O) [55]. The Co 2p XPS spectra of ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ (Fig. 6d) can be fitted into zero valence cobalt $(\mathsf{C o}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ (779.1 eV) and $\mathtt{C o}2\mathtt{p}1/2$ $(793.6~\mathrm{eV})_{,}^{\cdot}$ and oxidation state cobalt $(\mathsf{C o}^{n+}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ $781.5~\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathsf{C o}^{n+}\ 2\mathsf{p}1/2$ $(797~\mathrm{eV})_{,}^{,}$ with corresponding shakeup satellites (Co 2p3/2 (784.5 eV), $\mathtt{C o}^{n+}2\mathtt{p}3/2$ (787.7 eV), $\mathsf{C o}^{n+}2\mathsf{p}1/2$ (804.7 eV)). The surface of some metallic Co NPs may be in the oxidation state due to its exposure in ambient air. The Raman band intensity at about $680~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is a characteristic peak of oxidized cobalt species,[56] increases with the increase of the Co/Fe molar ratio (Fig. S7). This result also can be confirmed by the TG analysis, where the weight loss rate of the first period is in the order of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15)}>\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}>\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ (Fig. S8) due to the catalytic combustion of metallic cobalt. Thus, rational Co/Fe molar ratio is crucial to obtain integrated carbon substrate and active ORR sites including of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ and nonmetal functional groups.
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves were firstly executed in both $0_{2^{-}}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte to assess electrocatalytic properties of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ and different ${\mathsf{F C@N C s}}$ . As shown in Fig. 7a, distinct cathodic peaks could be recognized for ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ in $0_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte while no such peaks appear in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -saturated medium, manifesting all the ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ possess activity towards ORR reaction. A similar cathodic peak is also found in the
CV of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. S9a), which proves that N-doped carbon materials with $\mathsf{C-N}$ centers have ORR activity. However, the $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ samples have more positive potential of the cathodic peak $_{.0.81\mathrm{~V~}}$ for FC@NCs-3.15, 0.86 V for $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ and $0.83\mathrm{~V~}$ for ${\mathrm{FC@NCs-}}$ 5.15) than that of $_{0.71\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $\mathsf{N@C s}$ . This is because additional to the $C{\-}-\mathsf{N}$ active sites (e.g. pyridinic N), the embedded ${\cal C}0_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ alloys and Co NPs in ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ are also contributed to the ORR activities [57–65]. Among them, $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ gives the most positive cathodic potential close to that of $\sf P t/C$ (Fig. S9b) as it has relatively high pyridinic N content as well as uniformly distributed and well exposed ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{Fe}}_{7}$ sites compared with the other two. In addition, anodic peaks at around $1.02\mathrm{~V~}$ are also observed for the ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples, especially for $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ . The $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ sample requires $_{0.46\mathrm{~V~}}$ overpotential to reach the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. S9c). Thus, the $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ can potentially be used as both ORR and OER electrocatalyst [66–68]. The OER activity may root in the introduction of graphitic N with confined metallic Co NPs in carbon matrix and the mild oxidation state of some Co NPs [69,70]. The ORR activities of these catalysts were further studied by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV). After comparing the LSV polarization curves at different rotating speeds (Fig. S10 and Fig. 7b), $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ exhibits the most positive onset potential $\left(\mathrm{E_{onset}}\right)$ than the other two $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ due to the sufficient active sites. At low rotating speeds $400\sim900$ RPM), the limiting current density follows an order as: $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15<FC@NCs-3.15<FC@NC}$ s-4.15, since $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ and FC@NCs-4.15 have more integrated and continuous carbon network than $\mathrm{FC@NCs-5.15}$ . When the rotating speed reaches above 1225 RPM, the limiting current density of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ is slightly higher than that of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ . This is because $\mathrm{FC@NCs-3.15}$ has higher carbon content than $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ , which may limit the mass transport (e.g. $0_{2}$ and electrolyte diffusion) to the surface of reaction sites. Such a limitation can be effectively reduced at high rotating speed. The limiting current densities are elevated correspondingly with the increase of rotating speed for all the catalysts, (Fig. 7c and Fig. S11) due to the shortened path and enhanced mass transport rate at the electrode surface [71,72]. At 1600 RPM, $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ exhibits a $\mathrm{E_{onset}}$ of $0.94\mathrm{~V~}$ a half-wave potential $\left(\mathsf{E}_{1/2}\right)$ of $0.84\mathrm{~V~}$ and limiting current density of $4.7\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is comparable with some recently reported electrocatalysts (Table1) [73–79]. The Koutecky–Levich (K–L) cuves for $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ calculated from LSV polarization curves at various speeds are presented in Fig. 7d. The sustaining linearity relation between $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}^{-1}$ and $\boldsymbol{\upomega}^{-1/2}$ in the potential range of $0.3\mathrm{-}0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ suggests the oxygen reduction over the $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ catalyst abides by first-order reaction kinetics. In addition, CV and LSV tests were also carried out on the $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ sample and $\sf P t/C$ in the 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte (Fig. S12). The $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ sample displays certain ORR activity in acidic medium but it is inferior to that of $\sf P t/C$ . The main reason is that transition metal NPs (ORR active sites) in $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ could be etched by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . The XRD pattern of acid etched $\mathsf{F C@N C S-4.15-S}$ sample is shown in Fig. S13a. Compared with that of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ , the characteristic diffraction peaks of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ alloys are disappeared completely and the peaks of $\upbeta$ -Co become weaker, indicating the successful removal of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ NPs and partial Co NPs. The CV test results in Fig. S13b show that the cathodic peak potential of FC@NCs-4.15-s is more negative than that of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ . The LSV curves in Fig. S13c display the limiting current densities of $\mathsf{F C@N C S-4.15-S}$ is obviously lower than those of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ (Fig. 7c). Thus, the ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ particles have great contribution to the ORR performance of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ . On the other hand, the ORR performance of $\mathsf{F C@N C S-4.15-S}$ is still superior to that of $\mathsf{N@C s}$ (Fig. 9Sa), which manifests that the Co NPs may also positively affect the ORR activity. The contributions of Co NPs may be derived from the electron density modification of the surrounding graphene layers arisen from Co cores,[80–81] as the graphene layer wrapped Co NPs are relatively not easy be etched by HCl compared with the ones without cover layers. In addition, the anodic peak of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ is also reduced after acid etching (Fig. S13b) due to the destruction of the mild oxidation state of some Co NPs.
## 4. Conclusions
In short, N-doped graphene-like carbon nanosheets were introduced into the synthesis process of the FeCo-ZIF and the obtained products underwent a high-temperature carbonization to prepare a series of $\mathsf{F C@N C s}$ catalysts. The microstructure (e.g. active sites and carbon matrix) of the final materials can be adjusted by the initial $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}$ mole ratio. With an appropriate molar ratio $(\mathrm{Fe}/\$ $\mathsf{C o}=1/4.15)$ , the carbon network of the catalyst is integrated and continuous and the ORR active metal NPs wrapped with thin graphene layers are uniformly embedded this carbon matrix. The homogeneous distribution of ${\sf C o}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{7}$ NPs, the electron density modification from Co NPs to outer graphene layers and high pyridinic N content in $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ ensure the sufficient actives for ORR reaction, while the hierarchical pore structure with large surface area, low carbon content and high graphitic N component guarantee the efficient mass transfer to active sites and maintain the good electronic conductivity simultaneously. As a result, $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ exhibits superior ORR performance compared with the other two ${\mathrm{FC@NCs}}$ samples, and the stability and methanol crossover tolerance of $\mathrm{FC@NCs-4.15}$ in alkaline medium are even better than commercial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}$ In short, this research offers a controllable approach to prepare non-noble metal hybrid materials for electrochemical energy conversion.
